Check the XAML/DirectX interop header in the WinRT d3d11 probe

The d3d11 plugin probes for UWP (WINAPI_PARTITION_APP) support with a
cxx.compiles() snippet and, when the probe passes, compiles the WinRT window
sources gstd3d11window_corewindow.cpp / gstd3d11window_swapchainpanel.cpp.
Both of them include <windows.ui.xaml.media.dxinterop.h>:

  ../src/sys/d3d11/gstd3d11window_corewindow.cpp:32:10: fatal error:
    windows.ui.xaml.media.dxinterop.h: No such file or directory
  ../src/sys/d3d11/gstd3d11window_swapchainpanel.cpp:32:10: fatal error:
    windows.ui.xaml.media.dxinterop.h: No such file or directory

That is a Windows SDK XAML/DirectX interop header which the mingw-w64 headers
do not ship, so the probe currently reports a false positive: it only tests
"is this a Windows 10+ UWP-capable target", never "can the WinRT window sources
actually be compiled".

Whether the probe passes is decided by the prefix's default _WIN32_WINNT, which
is 0xA00 in UCRT64 but 0x601 in MINGW64 (_mingw.h).  The snippet's
`#if (WINVER < 0x0A00)` check therefore fails on mingw64 and passes on ucrt64 --
which is exactly why this fails on msys2-ucrt64 and not on msys2-mingw64.

Adding the missing header to the probe makes it test what actually matters.
The probe then correctly reports NO for MinGW (header absent) and still YES for
MSVC (the Windows SDK ships it), so only the Win32 desktop window path is built
here.  GCC loses nothing: the UWP CoreWindow/SwapChainPanel sinks are not
usable from a Win32 desktop application.

Same approach as MSYS2's mingw-w64-gst-plugins-bad 0004-check-d3d11-header.patch.

--- a/gst-libs/gst/d3d11/meson.build
+++ b/gst-libs/gst/d3d11/meson.build
@@ -86,6 +86,7 @@
 if runtimeobject_lib.found() and d3dcompiler_lib.found()
   d3d11_winapi_app = cxx.compiles('''#include <winapifamily.h>
       #include <windows.applicationmodel.core.h>
+      #include <windows.ui.xaml.media.dxinterop.h>
       #include <wrl.h>
       #include <wrl/wrappers/corewrappers.h>
       #include <d3d11_4.h>
